 By Neil Irwin and Courtenay Brown &middot; Nov 18, 2026 
 
 
 Between Neil's interview with New York Fed president John Williams yesterday afternoon and other scattered commentary from top officials, the central bank's path forward is getting a little bit clearer. More below.

 Plus, the mixed messages in this morning's housing data. 🏠 Today's newsletter, edited by George Moriarty and copy edited by Katie Lewis, is 883 words, a 3.5-minute read. 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: The Fed&#x27;s next moves come into focus 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 New York Fed president John C. Williams at yesterday's Axios BFD conference. Photo: Sam Popp for Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Get ready for an interest rate hike in three weeks' time. Then probably a couple more early next year. But pump the brakes on talk of a supersized rate hike — and keep an open mind about what 2027 policy looks like.

 That is the upshot of comments from Federal Reserve leadership in recent days that show officials relatively unfazed by the bumpy last few weeks in financial markets — so far, at least. The big picture: There is broad consensus among policymakers that inflation is the predominant concern right now, and the combination of robust growth, a solid job market and continued price pressure demands higher rates.

 The tightening in credit markets and drop in the stock market over the last month is getting close attention in the halls of the Fed, but so far is viewed more as a constructive repricing of risk. Top officials' disagreements are less about the direction of travel and more about the speed — and how to talk about it. Driving the news: "The economy is strong; it's strengthening, and inflation is stubbornly high," Williams said at the Axios BFD conference yesterday. "And we need to get it down," he said, adding that the Fed has "work to do" to bring inflation to 2%.

 He noted economic strength driven by the AI investment surge and robust consumer spending, saying that "if those continue, I think that's a factor that will argue that we may need to raise rates more." As to whether the wobbling stock market and stresses in AI-related debt markets amount to a tightening of conditions that might mitigate the need for rate hikes, he said that "financial conditions were incredibly positive just a few months ago," and that what we've seen is "more of a normalization of financial conditions which are still pretty supportive of growth." The intrigue: Williams poured cold water on the notion of raising rates by a half-percentage point, saying, "I don't think the situation calls for dramatic action."

 Governor Christopher Waller has raised that possibility, and did so again this morning. "I'm very, very concerned about inflation. It's kind of headed in the wrong direction, and it's spreading out," Waller said on CNBC's Squawk Box. "If you really want to get it down, you've got to start taking more aggressive actions." "You might need to, like, shock people," Waller said. Between the lines: Waller's argument is that under chairman Kevin Warsh's new regime with less explicit guidance about future policy action, the Fed needs to act more aggressively to send messages.

 In other words, if you're going to talk less, you may need your policy actions to do more of the talking for you. "If you're not going to signal this very clearly to markets" that a January rate hike is on the way, "my view is then you have to go to [half a percent] in December to put it in place and get it done." Yes, but: There's not much evidence Waller is winning the internal argument. Two hawkish presidents of regional Fed banks, Beth Hammack of Cleveland and Alberto Musalem of St. Louis, have each spoken this week about a string of smaller rate increases being justified.

 The bottom line: Wobbling financial markets aren't worrying the Fed too much yet, but high inflation readings are. Gradualism is the most likely response, not break-the-glass action.

 
 
