 2. Home construction rebounds, plans plunge 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: NAHB/Wells Fargo Housing Market Index ; Note: A reading above 50 indicates that more builders view housing market conditions as good than poor; Chart: Courtenay Brown/Axios Homebuilders are pulling back on plans for new construction, even as the number of homes they actually started building rebounded last month.

 Why it matters: The disconnect suggests the housing market's troubles are far from over, with builders bracing for weaker demand and higher costs.

 Elevated mortgage rates, rising construction costs and increasingly pessimistic builders threaten to prolong that weakness into next year. By the numbers: Housing starts rebounded by nearly 10% last month, recovering from a particularly weak September. But the more forward-looking data suggests construction will weaken in the months ahead.

 Building permits fell 7.4% to an annualized 1.3 million in October — the lowest level since May 2020, during pandemic shutdowns — led by a sharp pullback in single-family home construction. Between the lines: The shrinking pipeline of future construction mirrors a historic deterioration in homebuilder sentiment.

 Builder confidence fell to its lowest level since 2012 this month. About 46% of builders reported cutting prices, the highest share since the pandemic. Prospective buyer traffic fell to the lowest level since COVID and the lowest since 2015, excluding the pandemic. What they're saying: "Stepped-up immigration enforcement is keeping legal workers off job sites, deepening labor shortages and driving up costs," Bill Owens, chairman of the National Association of Home Builders, said in a release .

 "Higher fuel prices are prompting subcontractors to raise their rates sharply, and with fewer buyers in the market, more builders are scaling back construction," Owens said. The bottom line: The risk is that weaker construction becomes a more persistent drag on economic growth while making the nation's housing shortage harder to address.

 
 
